    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2888.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 719,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to truly understand water, we have to look at it through a few different lenses: its physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (Why Water is Weird and Wonderful)\nWater is one of the most unique and unusual substances in the universe. \n\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of the way its molecules are shaped (forming a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a negative charge on the oxygen side), water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances—like salt, sugar, and minerals. This is why rivers carry nutrients to the oceans and why our bodies can transport vitamins through our blood.\n* **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float in a glass, and it is crucial for life on Earth: if ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet. \n* **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by the oceans.\n* **Distribution:** Roughly 96.5% of Earth's water is saline (ocean water). Only 2.5% is freshwater, and most of *that* is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and ponds.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed system called the water cycle. It evaporates from the oceans into the vapor, cools and condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into the oceans via rivers.\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nSimply put, **life as we know it cannot exist without water.**\n* **Biology:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. It acts as a lubricant for joints, regulates body temperature through sweat, and flushes out waste. \n* **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species of plants and animals, from the deepest ocean trenches to high-altitude mountain streams.\n* **Agriculture and Civilization:** Human history is built around water. Ancient civilizations (like those in Mesopotamia, Egypt, and the Indus Valley) flourished along major rivers because water is required to grow crops and sustain communities.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with profound properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, a sculptor of landscapes, a regulator of climate, and the essential ingredient that makes biological life possible."
